     "rate": "+0%",
     "text": "Thousands of rough drafts enter the Capitol every single year, but most of them vanish inside committee rooms. People think writing a law is a quick signature process, but bills must survive endless committee reviews and floor debates in both houses before reaching the desk. Measures require approval from both the House and the Senate in identical form before heading to the president. If the president takes no action for ten days, the bill automatically becomes law, so inaction can overrule a veto. Congress can override a presidential veto with votes from both chambers."
